abolism and that  genetics do play a role here  but aside from getting some genetic  tests which may or may not be accurate  what is the best way to actually  determine  a person's needs well in clinical  settings a dietitian would most  often use predictive equations to figure  out how many calories a person needs  based on their height weight age and  activity level  however as we know everyone has very  unique needs  and these predictive equations are far  from perfect  they are honestly most useful for  providing a quick estimate  for the average person but they don't  really give us  a precise figure for instance a  predictive equation  might estimate that you need 2 000  calories a day  but you might find that you just feel  better and have more energy when  consuming fewer or more calories  than what was estimated that calculation  also doesn't really take into  consideration variations within a person  and that some days you'll just need more  calories than others  due to subtle changes in activity  hormones  sleep patterns sickness etc this is why  it's so important to rely on your own  hunger and fullness cues  to guide your eating rather than  whatever an online calculator says  as your body will provide the best  indicator for your unique energy needs  and it seems like stephanie does that  which is great  if you really want to know how much a  person needs when it comes to calories  you would have to do a indirect  calorometry test  instead of looking at an individual's  height weight age and activity level  indirect calorometry estimates energy  needs by evaluating  gas exchange and measuring how much  oxygen the body requires  versus how much carbon dioxide it  produces indirect calorometry is  considered the gold  standard when it comes to measuring  energy needs however  it's very expensive and isn't available  in most clinical settings  never mind something that the average  person would ever have access to  with this in mind when we consider  step