y kind of underlying health condition  i mean  we are all born with superpowers and  being able to  very efficiently use fuel is likely one  of stephanie's  but just for interest in education's  sake i did want to discuss  some health conditions that may put an  individual  in a hyper-metabolic state  hyper-metabolism compared to just having  a generally fast metabolism like  maybe stephanie is defined as having an  elevated  resting energy expenditure of over a  hundred and ten percent  usually an individual will enter a  hypermetabolic state  when recovering from an injury a burn or  when they're struggling with cancer  pretty much any condition where your  metabolism is revved up to help heal  or recover your body as a result of  having an increased energy expenditure  hyper metabolism can lead to involuntary  weight loss  elevated heart rate and blood pressure  muscle weakness  shortness of breath excessive sweating  and  an increased need for calories while  hyper metabolism  can be a consequence of injuries and  cancer  it can also be a sign of some of these  underlying health conditions  so first is hyperthyroidism  hyperthyroidism is characterized by an  overactive thyroid gland which  overproduces thyroid hormones  causing an increase in metabolic  activity some common symptoms of  hyperthyroidism include  increased appetite frequent bowel  movements weight loss  irregular menstrual cycles hair loss and  restlessness  hyperthyroidism is often treated with  medication to stop the thyroid from  over producing thyroid hormones as well  as surgery to remove  part or all of the thyroid gland next  is graves disease so graves disease is  an  autoimmune disorder that also causes the  thyroid gland  to overproduce thyroid hormone in the  body and  is one of the most common causes of  hyperthyroidism  because of this graves disease and  hypothyroidism  share many of the same symptoms graves  disease is also eight times more common  in women  than in men and has a very strong  genetic component  treatment options for graves disease are  likewise similar to those of  hyperthyroidism  since they are so strongly linked now  third is eating disorder  recovery eating disorder recovery can  also result in a temporary state of  hyper metabolism during weight  restoration  as the body recovers from being in a  prolonged period of starvation  this means that individuals recovering  from eating disorders like  anorexia who experience hyper metabolism  require substantially more calories to  restore their bodies back to baseline  i really liked what i heard stephanie  bettermore say in a recent video of hers  to explain her  high calorie needs when going all in  where she said that after being in a  caloric deficit for so long  she really had to kind of pay back the  debt and  also pay interest at the very start of  her journey  generally an individual's metabolism  will go back to normal  about three to six months after they  fully weight restore  then there's lipodystrophy so  lipodystrophy is  not a clinical manifestation of hyper  metabolism  but is instead an abnormality in the way  that the body uses and stores  fat some individuals with lipodystrophy  lack  fat cells so they can appear very  muscular  and defined however leptin which is the  satiety hormone that tells us when we're  full  is made in fat cells so because  individuals with lipodystrophy  lack fat cells they do not create enough  leptin  this means that they can eat large  quantities of food  without actually knowing when they're  full and because their body doesn't  store fat where most of us do in our  bellies  and our bums fat can accumulate in the  liver  which can lead to fatty liver disease  insulin resistance  and high cholesterol oftentimes these  individuals  may have to actually inject leptin  hormone to help curb their hunger  and protect their liver from the  accumulation of too much fat  and finally up for discussion is  pancreatic insufficiency  so pancreatic insufficiency doesn't  really result in hypermetabolism per se  but it's still worth mentioning here so  the pancreas not only helps to regulate  blood sugar levels but it also plays a  really important role  in digestion by creating digestive  enzymes  however when the pancreas stops  producing enzymes to support digestion  the body is unable to properly digest  and absorb fats  because fats are unable to be absorbed  by the body without digestive enzymes  food kind of passes through the body  undigested  which can result in extreme weight loss  this can also lead to vitamin deficiency  especially those fat soluble vitamins  like vitamin a d e and k  in these cases it's often recommended to  take an oral pancreatic enzyme  supplement  to support digestion and prevent  malnourishment now when i brought  stephanie's case  anonymously to my dietitian practice  group everyone was like spitballing all  these potential clinical reasons  for stephanie's obviously very high  metabolism  it is definitely super interesting as a  clinician to  go into diet detective mode with  thousands of really  really highly educated and experienced  professionals  obviously none of us know stephanie's  actual case so  all of this was totally hypothetical for  the purpose of discussion  and learning but one thing that did  leave pretty much everyone a bit  perplexed was the sheer variation in  caloric intake between  her different wedding day videos this  was the confusing thing for me too  back in my first video and still here  today as i said in my last video while  it's very normal to not eat the same  amount of calories  each day research suggests that the  average daily caloric variability for  women should be  plus or minus 25 of their total actual  energy needs  normally even if a person had a genetic  predisposition to a really high  metabolism  or was managing one of the conditions  that i just spoke to which can increase  metabolism we wouldn't see a 70 to  450 percent variation from day to day  alas the cool thing about nutrition is  that while i speak here to the masses  every single person is truly unique and  stephanie's a really cool case study to  follow  and ultimately if her body is giving  these broad fluctuating messages  for consumption and this is what feels  good to her  to me it's absolutely the right way to  eat bottom line  considering how stephanie talks about  food and her body  i do want to clarify that we can only  assume  that her unique energy needs are the  result of a fast metabolism  i did try to communicate this in my last  video but i admit  that the point was lost because i was  presenting the case  disproportionately from a restrict binge  lens  that is my ear and i am owning it  obviously we really can't understand  what  stephanie's specific energy needs are  without doing a full  and accurate energy needs assessment and  we also  can't rely on genetic testing alone as  it only gives us correlational data  which may or may not be accurate again i  want to apologize to stephanie and her  viewers  if my previous video sounded like i was  insinuating that stephanie was engaging  in problematic disorder behavior but i  do want to reiterate that the main  driving point  of my previous video was that it may be  triggering for some people  who interpret stephanie's videos in this  way having said that i also want to give  credit where credit is due  and give stephanie a huge virtual high  five  in acknowledgment that her videos may  also help others normalize certain  beliefs  and practices around food ultimately  it's all up to interpretation and  different people's histories and  struggles are likely going to inform  how these things are interpreted so  stephanie keep doing you  continue to reject diet mentality  continue to be a good role model for  those struggling with fear foods  and continue to fuel your body with  whatever  feels good to you clearly it's working  because you seem  very much at peace with your body and  with food  and on that note that is all for today's  video and another reminder to please be  kind in the comments  both here and on stephanie's channel if  you like this video be sure to give it  the thumbs up  leave me a comment below with who you'd  like to see me review next  subscribe to the channel and i'll see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye  [Music]
 

